{"input_text": "Question: I come to this limit: 
 $$\sum^{+\infty}_{k=1} \left(2+\frac{1}{k}-\frac{3}{k+1}\right)$$ 
 but I don't know how to calculate it. 
 Can you give me a hint? 

Answer: As proposed in the problem the series has flaws, namely, it diverges as $n \rightarrow \infty$. Consider the finite sum 
\begin{align}
\sum_{k=1}^{n} \left( 2 + \frac{1}{k} - \frac{3}{k+1} \right) 
\end{align}
which is evaluated as
\begin{align}
